  <h2>What's the real difference between a spreadsheet and QMS software?</h2>
  <p>
    A spreadsheet stores information. It doesn't enforce anything. Nothing stops two people saving different versions of the same file, nothing reminds anyone before a certificate expires, and nothing records who changed a cell last unless someone manually notes it. QMS software is built around the same document-control ideas ISO 9001 clause 7.5.3 describes, availability, protection, version control and retention, as features rather than habits you have to maintain by hand.
  </p>
  <p>
    This isn't only about documents. The same gap shows up in process records, such as nonconformances, change requests and supplier deviations, which most spreadsheet-based systems track as a row with a status column someone updates by hand. Software built for this designs the status, the approval step and the audit trail into the record itself, so the record can't quietly go stale the way a status cell can.
  </p>
  <p>
    That doesn't make a spreadsheet wrong. It makes it a manual system, where the discipline lives in a person's routine rather than in the tool. Whether that's good enough depends entirely on how much is riding on that routine never slipping.
  </p>
  <p>
    Take a simple example. A quality manager tracks certificates from three suppliers, Northwind Ingredients, Kestrel Packaging and Silverleaf Botanics, in a shared spreadsheet. Nothing stops a colleague opening an older copy from their downloads folder and editing it by mistake, and nothing tells anyone a certificate expired last week unless someone happens to scroll to that row. The information is all there. The checking still depends entirely on a person remembering to look.
  </p>

  <h2>Spreadsheets vs shared drives vs QMS software: a comparison</h2>
  <p>
    The table below compares the three approaches most SMEs use, across the things an ISO 9001 auditor and a busy quality manager care about most. None of these factors are hypothetical. They map directly onto what clause 7.5.3 expects for documented information, whichever tool you use to meet it.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Factor</th>
          <th>Spreadsheet / shared drive</th>
          <th>Dedicated QMS software</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Version control</td>
          <td>Manual, the last save wins, older copies often survive elsewhere</td>
          <td>Built in, a new version is recorded, the prior one kept in history</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Access control</td>
          <td>Whatever the file share or folder permissions allow</td>
          <td>Role-based, who can edit, approve or only view is set per person</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Audit trail</td>
          <td>None beyond a file's last-modified date</td>
          <td>Every upload, approval and status change logged automatically</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Reminders</td>
          <td>Only if someone remembers to check a date column</td>
          <td>Computed automatically from stored expiry dates, sent before they lapse</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Reporting</td>
          <td>Manual. Someone builds a pivot table or counts rows by hand</td>
          <td>Live dashboards and status counts, no manual recount needed</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Cost</td>
          <td>Usually "free" if you already pay for office software</td>
          <td>A monthly or annual subscription, typically priced per company or per tier rather than per feature</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Setup effort</td>
          <td>Minimal to start, but structure tends to erode over time without upkeep</td>
          <td>A short onboarding step, most vendors help migrate your existing registers</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>IT burden</td>
          <td>Low day to day, but you own backups, permissions and file-naming discipline</td>
          <td>Hosting, backups and updates are the vendor's responsibility</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>When is a spreadsheet still fine?</h2>
  <p>
    A spreadsheet is a reasonable, defensible choice when your scope is small: one site, one person maintaining the register, a handful of external documents and suppliers, and no pattern of documents slipping through unnoticed. Plenty of small companies run a clean, audit-ready spreadsheet for years without trouble, provided the discipline behind it, a fixed review date, a named owner, a habit of checking, never lapses.
  </p>
  <p>
    The whole system depends on one person's memory and availability. If that person is out sick, changes roles, or gets busy during a product launch, nothing in the spreadsheet itself will flag what's been missed.
  </p>
  <p>
    It's also worth being honest about what a spreadsheet can't do even when it's well maintained: it can't show an auditor, on request, exactly who approved a document and when, without you narrating the history yourself. For a small company that's rarely a problem, because the same one or two people who maintain the sheet can usually recall or reconstruct that history. As the team grows, that informal memory is the first thing to break down.
  </p>

  <h2>What are the signs you've outgrown spreadsheets?</h2>
  <p>
    A few patterns reliably show up once a spreadsheet-based system is under strain:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>More than one person edits the register, and you've had a version-conflict moment: two people working from different copies.</li>
    <li>You've had, or narrowly avoided, an expired supplier certificate or customer specification being used without anyone noticing in time.</li>
    <li>Preparing for an audit means someone spending a day or more reconstructing what happened, rather than pulling it up directly.</li>
    <li>Your supplier or customer count has grown enough that "just checking the sheet" no longer happens on a reliable schedule.</li>
    <li>You're being asked for evidence, such as an audit trail or a timestamped approval, that a spreadsheet cell can't produce.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    None of these mean the sheet failed you. They mean the scope has changed since you built it. See our <a href="/guides/control-of-external-documents">guide to controlling external documents</a> and <a href="/guides/supplier-certificate-tracking">tracking supplier certificates</a> for what a register needs to cover either way.
  </p>
  <p>
    A useful way to test where you stand: pick any document or supplier certificate at random and time how long it takes to answer three questions, namely is this the current version, who approved it, and when does it next need review. If that takes a search through email and a few phone calls rather than a few seconds, the system is already costing you more time than it looks like on paper.
  </p>
  <p>
    Run that test on a document that hasn't been touched in a while, too. The documents that catch companies out at audit are rarely the ones the quality manager checks every week. They're the one supplier certificate or one customer specification nobody has opened since it was filed, which is exactly the kind of gap a spreadsheet's structure can't surface on its own.
  </p>

  <h2>What does moving to QMS software involve?</h2>
  <p>
    Moving your existing registers across is usually the biggest perceived barrier and, in practice, the smaller part of the work. Most of what you're moving is already structured as rows in a spreadsheet. The bigger part is agreeing who owns what going forward, since software makes ownership visible rather than implicit.
  </p>
  <p>
    Expect three practical steps: importing your current documents and their metadata, deciding who gets editor access versus view-only access, and agreeing the expiry-reminder window you want, commonly 30 days. None of that requires downtime. Most SMEs keep their spreadsheet as a reference during the switch and stop updating it once the new register is confirmed complete.
  </p>
  <p>
    Cost is usually the first question and the easiest one to answer honestly. Weigh the monthly subscription against the time currently spent maintaining the spreadsheet, chasing suppliers by memory, and reconstructing history before an audit. For most small teams, that time cost is the larger of the two. It is less visible, because it's spread across a person's week rather than appearing as a single line on an invoice.
  </p>
